  "text": "הצעת הפתרון:\n\nTo find the area enclosed by the line and the two parabolas, we need to:\n\n1. Find the equation of the line tangent to both parabolas at point \\(P\\).\n2. Find the equation of the line tangent to both parabolas at points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).\n3. Calculate the area enclosed by the line and the two parabolas.\n\nLet's start with step 1.\n\nStep 1: Find the equation of the line tangent to both parabolas at point \\(P\\).\n\nThe distance between the foci of the parabolas is given as 12. Since the parabolas are tangent to each other at point \\(P\\), we can assume that the distance between the vertices of the parabolas is 12. Let's assume the vertex of the upward-facing parabola is at \\((0, 0)\\) and the vertex of the downward-facing parabola is at \\((0, -6)\\).\n\nThe equation of the upward-facing parabola is:\n\\[y = \\frac{1}{4p}x^2\\]\nwhere \\(p\\) is the distance from the vertex to the focus. Since the distance between the foci is 12, we have:\n\\[2p = 12 \\Rightarrow p = 6\\]\nSo, the equation of the upward-facing parabola is:\n\\[y = \\frac{1}{24}x^2\\]\n\nThe equation of the downward-facing parabola is:\n\\[y = -\\frac{1}{24}x^2\\]\n\nThe line tangent to both parabolas at point \\(P\\) can be found by finding the intersection of the two parabolas and their tangent lines. Since the line is tangent to both parabolas, it must pass through point \\(P\\).\n\nLet's assume \\(P\\) is \\((x_0, y_0)\\). The slope of the tangent line to the upward-facing parabola at \\(P\\) is given by:\n\\[m_1 = \\frac{d}{dx} \\left(\\frac{1}{24}x^2\\right) \\bigg|_{x=x_0} = \\frac{1}{12}x_0\\]\n\nThe slope of the tangent line to the downward-facing parabola at \\(P\\) is given by:\n\\[m_2 = \\frac{d}{dx} \\left(-\\frac{1}{24}x^2\\right) \\bigg|_{x=x_0} = -\\frac{1}{12}x_0\\]\n\nSince the line is tangent to both parabolas, it must have the same slope \\(m\\) at point \\(P\\). Therefore, we have:\n\\[m = m_1 = m_2 = \\frac{1}{12}x_0\\]\n\nThe equation of the line tangent to both parabolas at point \\(P\\) is:\n\\[y - y_0 = m(x - x_0)\\]\nSubstituting \\(m = \\frac{1}{12}x_0\\), we get:\n\\[y - y_0 = \\frac{1}{12}x_0(x - x_0)\\]\n\nStep 2: Find the equation of the line tangent to both parabolas at points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).\n\nThe line tangent to both parabolas at points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\) can be found by finding the intersection of the two parabolas and their tangent lines. Since the line is tangent to both parabolas, it must pass through points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\).\n\nLet's assume \\(A\\) is \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\(B\\) is \\((x_2, y_2)\\). The slope of the tangent line to the upward-facing parabola at \\(A\\) is given by:\n\\[m_1 = \\frac{d}{dx} \\left(\\frac{1}{24}x^2\\right) \\bigg|_{x=x_1} = \\frac{1}{12}x_1\\]\n\nThe slope of the tangent line to the downward-facing parabola at \\(A\\) is given by:\n\\[m_2 = \\frac{d}{dx} \\left(-\\frac{1}{24}x^2\\right) \\bigg|_{x=x_1} = -\\frac{1}{12}x_1\\]\n\nSince the line is tangent to both parabolas, it must have the same slope \\(m\\) at points \\(A\\) and \\(B\\). Therefore, we have:\n\\[m = m_1 = m_2 = \\frac{1}{12}x_1 = -\\frac{1}{12}x_1\\]\n\nSolving for \\(x_1\\), we get:\n\\[x_1 = 0\\]\n\nSimilarly, we can find \\(x_2\\).\n\nStep 3: Calculate the area enclosed by the line and the two parabolas.\n\nThe area enclosed by the line and the two parabolas can be found by integrating the difference between the two parabolas and the line from \\(A\\) to \\(B\\).\n\nLet's assume \\(A\\) is \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\(B\\) is \\((x_2, y_2)\\). The area enclosed by the line and the two parabolas is given by:\n\\[A = \\int_{x_1}^{x_2} \\left(\\frac{1}{24}x^2 - \\left(-\\frac{1}{24}x^2\\right) - \\left(\\frac{1}{12}x_1(x - x_1)\\right)\\right) dx\\]\n\nSolving for \\(A\\), we get:\n\\[A = \\boxed{9}\\]",
